{"name":"Hair stick / kanzashi / binyeo styling","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Kinuyo_Tanaka_-_Kanzashi_%281941%29.jpg/960px-Kinuyo_Tanaka_-_Kanzashi_%281941%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kinuyo_Tanaka_-_Kanzashi_(1941).jpg","caption":"Kinuyo Tanaka - Kanzashi (1941)","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Hair sticks are pins used to fasten and decorate hairstyles. The Japanese kanzashi and Korean binyeo are the best-known forms, worn to secure a bun or chignon while adding ornament.","They help appearance by giving long hair a tidy, styled shape and a decorative accent, and historically signalled things like marital status or season. Today they are also worn as a fashion accessory."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["The pin is typically placed through a twisted bun or topknot to hold it; ornamented types add flowers, beads or carved ends. Kanzashi come as sticks, combs and folded-silk flowers."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Generally low risk as hair accessories. Pin ends are rigid and pointed, and tortoiseshell was historically used."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Kinuyo_Tanaka_-_Kanzashi_%281941%29.jpg/960px-Kinuyo_Tanaka_-_Kanzashi_%281941%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Kinuyo_Tanaka_-_Kanzashi_%281941%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Kinuyo Tanaka - Kanzashi (1941)","license":"Public domain","creator":"Shōchiku","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kinuyo_Tanaka_-_Kanzashi_(1941).jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Hanako_with_December_kanza